A struct member carries its signedness.

build_member callocs the member record and copies the declared type's SIZE
into it, but never its is_signed. Every struct member therefore reported as
unsigned, whatever it was declared:

    struct S { int a; char b; unsigned c; };
        a  sz=4 sgn=0      should be signed
        b  sz=1 sgn=0      should be signed
        c  sz=4 sgn=0      correctly unsigned

The member read chooses its instruction from that flag --

    emit_out(load_value(i->size, i->is_signed));

-- so it emitted the zero-extending ldr_w0 where int needs ldrsw, and a
negative value written through `p->c` came back as its unsigned bit pattern:
-1 stored 0xFFFFFFFF and read back 0x00000000FFFFFFFF.

    struct S { int c; };
    int main(void){ struct S s; struct S* p; p=&s;
                    p->c = 0 - 1; if(p->c != 0 - 1) return 1; return 0; }
    gcc 0, micro-c 1

WHILE `int` WAS EIGHT BYTES this could not show: an eight-byte load has nothing
to extend, so the flag never reached the emitted code. At four bytes it decides
correctness.

WHAT IT BLOCKED. tcc pushes a fresh struct with c = -1 and then asks

    if (s->c != -1 && ...) tcc_error("struct/union/enum already defined");

The comparison failed, so every anonymous struct in tccdefs.h -- beginning with
aarch64's __builtin_va_list, at <command line>:56 -- reported as already
defined. mc-tcc rejected EVERY program it was handed, including
`int main(void){ return 7; }`, with

    <command line>:53: error: struct/union/enum already defined

HOW IT WAS FOUND. Dump the <command line> buffer to see what line 53 is; read
the condition that raises the error rather than guessing at it; tag all 22
load_value call sites and compile `p->c` to see which one fires; print size and
is_signed at that site. Four measurements, no hypotheses.

MEASURED: negative int, char and pointer-adjacent members all match gcc, and
unsigned members stay unsigned. difftest stays 84/85 -- no regression.
mc-tcc COMPILES: `-c seven.c` returns 0 with a 1,038-byte object, where it
previously rejected everything.

STILL FAILING: programs using pointer-into-array walks and function-pointer
struct members segfault at RUN time (the 05 and 11 shapes, and difftest's
14-function-pointer-member). Those are codegen, not this.

--- a/cc_types.c
+++ b/cc_types.c
@@ -744,6 +744,34 @@ struct type* build_member(struct type* last, int offset)
 	{
 		i->size = member_type->size;
 	}
+
+	/* AND ITS SIGNEDNESS, which nothing here copied.
+	 *
+	 * build_member callocs the record, so is_signed started 0 and stayed
+	 * there: EVERY struct member reported as unsigned, whatever it was
+	 * declared. The member read then chose the zero-extending load --
+	 *
+	 *     emit_out(load_value(i->size, i->is_signed));    ldr_w0, not ldrsw
+	 *
+	 * -- so a negative value written through `p->c` read back as its unsigned
+	 * bit pattern: -1 stored 0xFFFFFFFF and returned 0x00000000FFFFFFFF.
+	 *
+	 * While `int` was eight bytes an eight-byte load had nothing to extend
+	 * and signedness never reached the emitted code. At four bytes it decides
+	 * correctness.
+	 *
+	 * tcc pushes a fresh struct with c = -1 and then asks
+	 *
+	 *     if (s->c != -1) tcc_error("struct/union/enum already defined");
+	 *
+	 * so every anonymous struct in tccdefs.h -- beginning with aarch64's
+	 * __builtin_va_list at <command line>:56 -- reported as already defined,
+	 * and every program our tcc was handed failed to compile.
+	 *
+	 * An ARRAY member keeps the element's signedness, which is what a read of
+	 * one element needs. A function pointer has none to carry. */
+	i->is_signed = member_type->is_signed;
+
 	member_size = i->size;
 
 	return i;
